hostap.git test tools
---------------------

The tests directory with its subdirectories contain number of tools used
for testing wpa_supplicant and hostapd implementations.

hwsim directory contains the test setup for full system testing of
wpa_supplicant and hostapd with a simulated radio (mac80211_hwsim). See
hwsim/READM and hwsim/vm/README for more details.

MACsec rollover regressions
--------------------------

make -C tests test-macsec_cp_wedge test-macsec_rollover
tests/test-macsec_cp_wedge
tests/test-macsec_rollover

The rollover test compiles the production KaY, CP, SecY wrappers, key
derivation, packet verification and event-loop code. Only packet transport
and hardware callbacks are replaced. It requires the OpenSSL development
library already used by OpenSSL-based wpa_supplicant builds.

SAKs, including the in-flight proposal, belong to the port rather than a CA.
A principal change preserves their identities, ANs, packet numbers and CP
phase. One local proposal is allowed at a time; missing DistSAKs are
retransmitted with the same identity, and occupied ANs are never predicted
to become free. A new negotiating actor cannot reissue another actor's KI.
Fresh key-use observations belong to the peer's shared receive SCI, so
removing the CA that carried a report does not destroy that evidence.
Absent or empty standby reports neither erase nor extend its lifetime.

TX selection requires fresh, full-KI-and-AN-matched RX readiness from every
live principal peer, including at initial establishment. Peer TX may still
use the old key. Neither the transmit deadline nor a disabled controlled
port substitutes for readiness. The normal transmit deadline instead permits
safe abandonment of an unreceived proposal while retaining the current TX SA.
The key server keeps a fresh-distribution request pending until the abandoned
candidate has drained and its slot is released.

Pre-TX recovery uses the existing ABANDON state. Fresh reports must show that
every live principal peer can receive the retained key and that none
transmits the candidate before an inherited proposal is abandoned. After
withdrawal the proposal cannot be redistributed, even if its originating
CA becomes principal again while RX is draining. Candidate RX remains
installed for the normal drain delay, and deletion requires fresh evidence.
Once TX selection is requested, neither an acknowledgment delay nor a driver
error makes the candidate safe to abandon: hardware programming may already
be queued. The candidate is preserved across principal changes; CP never
selects the previous TX SA again. Unconfirmed TX selection is bounded by an
explicit timeout and controlled-port restart, not rollback.

After TX completes, both key server and follower retain old RX until every
live principal peer freshly reports TX with the exact new KI and AN.
RX readiness alone, stale reports and a queued replacement cannot retire
the old key. A twenty-second failsafe retires it with an explicit warning
if TX confirmation never arrives; this escape is not guaranteed hitless.
Failure to arm the timeout explicitly closes the controlled port and
restarts negotiation. The three-second drain still applies to abandoned
pre-TX candidates, not to normal old-key retirement.

Changing the elected key server does not reset installed or in-flight SAs.
CP's chgdServer signal follows a valid SAK distribution from an SCI not
represented by existing SAs (802.1X-2020, 12.2). A known peer can become key
server without losing the current keys or their packet numbers. A follower
promoted during READY applies the key-server readiness gate to the retained
proposal rather than waiting for the former server to transmit.

Unresolved pre-TX recovery
is bounded by the existing retirement failsafe and explicitly restarts
negotiation with the controlled port closed; it does not fabricate success.
If every peer instead commits to the candidate during recovery, CP selects
that existing TX SA and completes the rollover without resetting its PN.

Only the principal sends SAK Use. Other CAs stay live through authenticated,
replay-checked recent MI/MN echoes; omitted standby SAK Use is not a peer
failure. Missing principal SAK Use requests bounded key-progress repair
without pretending that an authenticated, responsive peer has disappeared.

Cases cover missed-DistSAK readiness, pre-TX recovery, forward-only TX,
queued selection and activation errors, RX drain and its deadline,
peer-confirmed old-RX retirement and its twenty-second timeout for both
roles, key-server role changes,
validated distribution from a new SCI,
same-candidate retransmission, originating-CA deletion, overlapping incoming
SAKs, occupied ANs, failed activation, signed SAK-Use omission, stale echoes,
replay, invalid ICVs, and matching a full KI in either peer key-use slot.
These are offline regressions, not hardware completion or lossless-data-path
qualification. SONiC driver wait/acknowledgment semantics are unchanged.
If fresh peer RX readiness is unavailable, CP does not request candidate TX.
Missing SAK Use is neither readiness nor proof that an installed key is unused.


Build testing
-------------

wpa_supplicant and hostapd support number of build option
combinations. The test scripts in the build subdirectory can be used to
verify that various combinations do not break the builds. More
configuration examples can be added there
(build-{hostapd,wpa_supplicant}-*.config) to get them included in test
builds.

# Example
cd build
./run-build-tests.h


Fuzz testing
------------

Newer fuzz testing tools are under the fuzzing directory. See
fuzzing/README for more details on them. The following text describes
the older fuzz testing tools that are subject to removal once the same
newer tools have the same coverage available.

Number of the test tools here can be used for fuzz testing with tools
like American fuzzy lop (afl-fuzz) that are designed to modify an
external file for program input. ap-mgmt-fuzzer, eapol-fuzzer,
test-eapol, test-json, test-tls, and test-x509 are examples of such
tools that expose hostap.git module functionality with input from a file
specified on the command line.

Here are some examples of how fuzzing can be performed:

##### JSON parser
make clean
CC=afl-gcc make test-json
mkdir json-examples
cat > json-examples/1.json <<EOF
{"a":[[]],"b":1,"c":"q","d":{"e":[{}]}}
EOF
afl-fuzz -i json-examples -o json-findings -- $PWD/test-json @@

Alternatively, using libFuzzer from LLVM:
make clean
make test-json LIBFUZZER=y
mkdir json-examples
cat > json-examples/1.json <<EOF
{"a":[[]],"b":1,"c":"q","d":{"e":[{}]}}
EOF
./test-json json-examples

##### EAPOL-Key Supplicant
make clean
CC=afl-gcc make test-eapol TEST_FUZZ=y
mkdir eapol-auth-examples
./test-eapol auth write eapol-auth-examples/auth.msg
afl-fuzz -i eapol-auth-examples -o eapol-auth-findings -- $PWD/test-eapol auth read @@

##### EAPOL-Key Authenticator
make clean
CC=afl-gcc make test-eapol TEST_FUZZ=y
mkdir eapol-supp-examples
./test-eapol supp write eapol-supp-examples/supp.msg
afl-fuzz -i eapol-supp-examples -o eapol-supp-findings -- $PWD/test-eapol supp read @@

##### TLS client
make clean
CC=afl-gcc make test-tls TEST_FUZZ=y
mkdir tls-server-examples
./test-tls server write tls-server-examples/server.msg
afl-fuzz -i tls-server-examples -o tls-server-findings -- $PWD/test-tls server read @@

##### TLS server
make clean
CC=afl-gcc make test-tls TEST_FUZZ=y
mkdir tls-client-examples
./test-tls client write tls-client-examples/client.msg
afl-fuzz -i tls-client-examples -o tls-client-findings -- $PWD/test-tls client read @@

##### AP management frame processing
cd ap-mgmt-fuzzer
make clean
CC=afl-gcc make
mkdir multi-examples
cp multi.dat multi-examples
afl-fuzz -i multi-examples -o multi-findings -- $PWD/ap-mgmt-fuzzer -m @@

##### EAPOL-Key Supplicant (separate)
cd eapol-fuzzer
make clean
CC=afl-gcc make
mkdir eapol-examples
cp *.dat eapol-examples
afl-fuzz -i eapol-examples -o eapol-findings -- $PWD/eapol-fuzzer @@

##### P2P
cd p2p-fuzzer
make clean
CC=afl-gcc make
mkdir p2p-proberesp-examples
cp proberesp*.dat p2p-proberesp-examples
afl-fuzz -i p2p-proberesp-examples -o p2p-proberesp-findings -- $PWD/p2p-fuzzer proberesp @@
mkdir p2p-action-examples
cp go*.dat inv*.dat p2ps*.dat p2p-action-examples
afl-fuzz -i p2p-action-examples -o p2p-action-findings -- $PWD/p2p-fuzzer action @@

##### WNM
cd wnm-fuzzer
make clean
CC=afl-gcc make
mkdir wnm-examples
cp *.dat wnm-examples
afl-fuzz -i wnm-examples -o wnm-findings -- $PWD/wnm-fuzzer @@
